Cobalt Strike’s Malleable C2 profiles allow adversaries to customize beacon behavior to evade detection by blending malicious traffic with legitimate protocols. While traditional HTTP/S beacons and DNS tunneling rely on overt misuse of protocols, Malleable C2 employs **protocol-level manipulation**, **metadata cloaking**, and **dynamic response obfuscation** to bypass network defenses.

---

### **Comparison of Evasion Techniques**

| **Technique**                     | **Traditional HTTP/S Beacon**                          | **DNS Tunneling**                                      | **Cobalt Strike Malleable C2**                         |
|-----------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------|
| **Protocol Used**                 | HTTP/S POST/GET with base64-encoded payloads          | DNS A, AAAA, TXT records with encoded exfil data       | Fully customizable HTTP/S, DNS, or direct TCP         |
| **Evasion Mechanism**             | Randomized User-Agents, URL paths, encryption (RC4/AES) | Split queries, subdomain encoding, fast flux DNS       | Protocol morphing (e.g., mimic Office 365, SAP, SharePoint) |
| **Metadata Cloaking**             | Limited (static User-Agents, predictable headers)     | None (DNS queries inherently noisy)                   | Full control over HTTP/S headers, TLS fingerprinting, and request timing |
| **Response Handling**             | Static payloads or encrypted blobs                     | None (one-way exfil)                                   | Dynamic responses (e.g., split second-stage DLLs), request chaining |
| **Defensive Bypass Strategy**     | Signature-based IDS/IPS (snort rules for "POST /upload") | DNS exfiltration filters (e.g., Cisco Umbrella)         | Mimics legitimate SaaS traffic (e.g., `outlook.office.com/api/login`) |

